    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Faults are non-planar at all scales. Recent contributions to the sinusoidal fault problem provide analytical and numerical models for stress perturbations and deformation; however, these models are based on assumptions that ultimately limit their utility. We use two-dimensional numerical solutions for quasi-static elastic boundary value problems to investigate sinusoidal cracks as models for faults in an otherwise homogeneous and isotropic elastic material. Our displacement discontinuity boundary element method incorporates a complementarity algorithm in order to include the appropriate contact boundary conditions on the fault. We create a scheme to ignore tip effects, which allows us to compare our work to earlier models. Evaluation of the deformation and local stress heterogeneity due to fault geometry shows that slip along non-planar faults may cause fault opening and secondary fractures to form, influencing local permeability and fluid transport. Failure in tension may occur along lee sides and failure in compression may occur along stoss sides. We agree with previous research which suggests that the nature and intensity of off-fault damage is spatially correlated with geometric irregularities.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
